// QUEST_STORY: an optional paragraph for the first and last quest of each storyline (chains of three or more), shown
// behind "More" in the quest dialog. Spoken by the quest giver; checked by tools/lorekeeper.js at the quest's level.
(function (root) {
  const D = root.D;
  D.QUEST_STORY = {
    a_new_threat: 'I have walked this valley for forty winters, and I never saw the cavekin this bold. They climb out of cracks in the stone that were not there last spring, as if something down below is pushing them up. Brunhall is a few stone walls and a stubborn lot of dwarves. Stubborn will not be enough if they keep coming.',
    stolen_journal: 'Nib has written in that journal for years: every storm, every cavekin seen, the name of every dwarf who stood a watch here. Skrell did not take it to read it. He took it because he knows it matters to us. You have already thinned his whelps in that cave. Now show the old troll that this valley keeps what is its own.',
    report_kharanos: 'Old Halstein has watched the Grimtooth longer than any of us, and he keeps count of every camp they raise. If the trolls are pushing into our valley, they are pushing towards Bjornstad as well. Tell him what you saw in the cave, all of it. The pass is cold and the wolves are hungry, so keep moving and keep your hood up.',
    frostmane_seers: 'You have seen how the trolls fight now, closer to the town every week. That is the seers\' doing. They sit by their fires and tell the Grimtooth that these mountains were theirs before the first dwarf cut stone, and the young ones believe every word. Perhaps it was so, once. It is our home now, and Bjornstad will not be given up to an old story.',
    road_dolanaar: 'Ithrenne sits on the road between this glade and Nyrwen, and Calen has watched over it since Greatbough first took root. He is a Druid of the Claw, and patient with the young, though he would never say so. Walk softly under the great boughs. The forest is still young, and not everything that grows in it has grown as we hoped.',
    oakenscowl_q: 'I knew Old Barkjaw when the Mossback were our friends. He brought his people to trade at our fires and sat with our druids through the long nights. Something has rotted in him since, and in all of them. You have seen what his warriors have become. If he falls, perhaps what is left of his tribe will remember who they were. I no longer expect it.',
    gnarlpine_shamans: 'The shamans were the tribe\'s healers once. They knew every root and spring on the island, and the young bearkin listened to them above all others. That is why the corruption spreads so fast: it speaks with the voices the Mossback trust most. You have fought their warriors at Rootdeep. The shamans are worse, because they believe they are helping.',
    report_razor_hill: 'Drogga has held Bonewall since we first came to this land. He is hard and he speaks little, but he does not waste warriors. The humans at Saltwall Keep watch our coast from their walls, and Bonewall is the first thing they see. Go to him with your head up. He will know soon enough what you are worth.',
    benedict_q: 'The insignias you brought me tell the story. These are not lost sailors. They are soldiers of Brineholt, and they built a keep on our shore as if the land were theirs to take. Benedict gives them their orders and their courage. Without him the keep is only stone and frightened men. The peace between our peoples is thin. He has made it thinner.',
    report_bloodhoof: 'Ossa Village is the heart of our people on the plains, and Tarro is the son of our chief. He is young, but the Grass Mother gave him his father\'s patience. Go down the mountain slowly and look at the land as you pass. Greensward feeds us and asks little in return. Whoever harms it answers to every hornfolk.',
    last_poachers: 'The Ashpelt do not hunt to eat. They take the hides and leave the rest for the vultures, and the herds we have followed for generations grow thinner every season. You drove them back once already, for Tarro. These are the last of them, and the most stubborn. When the rock is quiet, the longnecks will graze there again. I would like to see that before winter.',
    report_gryan: 'I have asked Kingsmere for soldiers to send west. Every answer comes back the same: the army is needed elsewhere, and Longfield must look after itself. So the farmers did. Bram Oakhollow was a soldier once, and he has turned their pitchforks into a militia. He needs every blade he can get. I cannot spare him mine, but I can send him you.',
    wanted_highwaymen: 'You cleared the road once, and the carts are moving again. But the ones who still ride are the old hands, the ones who taught the rest to rob. I have watched them from the hills for weeks. They know every gully between here and Fenwick, and they never camp twice in one place. When they are gone, the farmers can drive without looking over their shoulders.',
    unsent_letter_q: 'Think of what you have seen since you came to Warrick\'s Rise: the trappers, the pathstalkers, the orders, Fenwick burned to its foundations. Now a sealed letter, and a ship. Stolen coin does not build a warship. Someone is paying Blackwell, and whoever it is sits a long way from Longfield. Finish him. What he carries may tell us who.',
    disrupt_attacks: 'Dustfort feeds half the Scrublands. Every caravan from Vazhrak passes under these walls, and every one the centaurs take means a hungry outpost somewhere down the road. The Galloran say this land is theirs by their fathers\' blood, and they mean to take it back one wagon at a time. Show them the Krugar does not pay tolls.',
    kolkar_leaders: 'You have killed his drudges and his wranglers, and still Rhaz the Herdbreaker sends raiders to our roads. He is no fool. He knows the Krugar is stretched thin out here, and he is waiting for us to tire. Every clan of the Galloran watches the Sourwater Oasis to see who is stronger. When he falls, they will remember what they saw.',
    lushwater_scout: 'I have hunted raptors since I was a child, and the ones at Greenwell are different. They hunt in packs, they learn where the caravans stop, and they come back to the same places. That is not hunger. That is patience. Look before we fight. I want to know how many there are, and how clever.',
    lushwater_raptors: 'Vesha tells me you have hunted the scytheclaws already. The drivers are grateful, but they still come in with torn wagons and fewer dustbacks than they left with. They sit at my fire and drink and do not talk. An innkeeper learns to read silence. When the oasis is clear, perhaps they will tell stories again instead of counting the dead.',
    rusty_golems: 'Those golems were built to plough and to reap. My family saved two seasons of coin to hire one, back when Longfield still had coin to save. Now they walk in circles on the Rustfield and crush whatever is left of the crops. Salma cries when she sees them from the window. I do not want revenge. I just want my fields back.',
    dead_acre_sweep: 'Wenham told me what you did for his fields. The rusty golems were old machines gone wrong. The reapers are new, and new machines cost money the Grey Hoods should not have. Somebody is lending it to them. I cannot stop whoever that is, not yet. I can stop the machines. When the Rustfield is quiet, the farmers can plant again, and Longfield can eat.',
    digsite_scout: 'The dwarves say they dig for old stones and the bones of dead kings. Perhaps they do. But they brought rifles and soldiers with their shovels, and they never asked whose hills they were digging in. Keldrun and Vazhrak are not at war. Soldiers with guns on Krugar land make that hard to remember. I want to know what they are really after.',
    digsite_sweep: 'From Hollow Tower I can see their fires every night, closer to Hoofbreak Hill than they were the month before. Grukk sends scouts and asks questions. I send warriors. You have seen their excavators at work, tearing the hills open as if the land owed them something. The Scrublands are Krugar land. The dwarves can dig in their own mountains.',
    baeldun_orders_q: 'You broke their soldiers, and still the dig goes on. That means someone above them wants it very badly. If Keldrun itself sent them, this is not a band of treasure hunters on our land. It is a choice made by a king. I must see those orders with my own eyes before I send word to Vazhrak. Words like these start wars.',
    windshear_scout: 'I have walked these mountains since I was a calf, and the earth has always spoken to me quietly. Now it screams. The goblins came with saws and fire and machines that walk like men, and they cut the forest as if it would grow back by morning. It will not. Go and see it for yourself. Then tell me I am wrong.',
    deforesters: 'You have stopped many of the loggers, and the goblins have answered the only way they know. What they cannot carry away, they burn. The smoke reaches Tallstone at night, and the young ones cough in their sleep. Ash will lie on the crag for years, and nothing will grow there until the rains have washed it clean.',
    renders_scout: 'The Cinderpeak have come down from the mountains before, but never in such numbers, and never to stay. They camp under Scorched Rock as if the valley were theirs. I have written to Kingsmere twice for soldiers and heard nothing back. Until someone answers, Longbridge defends itself. I need to know how many there are, and how well they are armed.',
    renegades: 'You have seen their outrunners on our roads. The renegades are worse. They burn barns, drive off the cattle and leave families with nothing to see the winter through. Some of those farmers held the line with me against the Krugar in the Long War. They should not have to do it again with pitchforks. Kingsmere has not answered. You did.',
    logging_orders_q: 'Goblins do nothing without a contract, and nothing on a contract comes cheap. Someone is paying the Deepgold Company to strip these mountains bare, and paying well. I want to know who. The deforesters carry their orders in their pockets, the way they carry everything of value. Paper remembers what goblins would rather forget.',
    pump_valves: 'The orders led you to Kettle, and you saw what they have done there. The lake was deep when my grandmother was young. Now the goblins pump it dry to feed their machines, and the streams below it run thin. Every creature in these hills drinks from that water. Let the mountain have back what is its own.',
    cragpool_patrol: 'Rakko tells me you have fought their operators at the dam. The goblins will not give up so easily. They count trees and water the way we count our ancestors, and they hate to lose a single one. If we leave the works standing, new crews will come with the next shipment. Break them so completely that the Deepgold Company decides these mountains cost too much.',
    galardell_scout: 'My brother is not a liar. He drinks, and he tells tall stories about the fish he caught, but he came home white as milk and would not say a word until morning. Small black dragons, he said, no bigger than dogs, and grass burned black around them. The marshal laughed. Please, just look. If he is wrong, I will make him apologise.',
    whelp_hunt: 'Aldren has his scales, and his letters to Kingsmere will be long ones. I am a soldier. I do not need proof, I need a plan. Whelps do not hatch by themselves in Dunmore Valley. Something laid those eggs and is keeping them warm, and it is bigger than anything this town has ever faced. We start with what we can kill.',
    charred_vale_scout: 'The Screaming Vale burned long before any of us came to Tallstone. Some say it was a war between the old spirits of the land. Some say it was worse. Nothing tall grows there even now. The harpies like it because nobody else will go near it. I want to know how many have gathered there, and why they have grown so bold.',
    storm_witches: 'You have fought the Redclaw on our patrol roads and seen how they hate everything that walks on the ground. The storm witches are the worst of them. When the sky is clear, they call it dark, and their lightning finds the driest trees first. The Vale burned once already. I will not watch the rest of these mountains follow it.',
    blackrock_orders_q: 'Orcs raiding farms is a misery. Orcs following written orders is a war. The Cinderpeak have officers now, and the officers have plans, and nobody in Kingsmere reads my reports. I have been magistrate of this town for many years, and I have never been so frightened for it. I want to know what they intend before they do it.',
    summoner_tomes: 'Watcher\'s Keep was ours. Our grandfathers built it to guard these hills against the orcs, and now the orcs sit behind its walls and call fire down on us from our own towers. You have seen the keep. Their fire has already taken two barns and a family\'s home. I want to know who taught the Cinderpeak magic like that, and what else they learned.',
    gathilzogg_q: 'You have read their orders, walked the keep and cut down the champions on its walls. All of it leads back to one orc. Uzbrak has burned this valley for years, and every time we drove his raiders off, he came back with more. When his head is in my hands, every orc in these hills will know Longbridge is not theirs. Perhaps Kingsmere will finally listen.',
    keep_patrol: 'Watcher\'s Keep was built to guard this valley. My grandfather helped to lay its stones. Now the orcs keep their war plans behind those walls, and you have seen for yourself how many hold them. Marshal Corwin says the champions you cut down were replaced within a day. So we keep them busy. An orc watching his own walls is an orc not coming down to the lake.',
    grimtotem_scout: 'The Sourhorn were our kin. They shared our fires and our songs. Then they decided the hornfolk should rule the land rather than listen to it, and they turned from the Grass Mother to do it. I can feel their totems in the ground from here, like a splinter under the skin. Go carefully. They will not treat you as a guest.',
    grimtotem_plans: 'Tamu of the Stone grieves for these people. I understand it, but grief does not guard a gate. You have seen their brutes on the path, and you know they mean to come here. A warrior without a plan dies brave. A warrior who has read his enemy\'s plan goes home. The mystics carry theirs. Bring it to me, and Tallstone will be ready for them.',
    grimtotem_mystics_q: 'The totems you brought me were carved with care. That is what troubles me most. Whoever made them knew our oldest prayers and turned each one inside out. The mystics were healers and seers once, the same as any of our elders. Now they spend their gifts on curses. I cannot break a curse faster than they carve one. It has to stop at the hand.',
    worgen_fangs: 'Hollin Grove was where Lanternby went for timber, and for a quiet walk when the day was done. Nobody walks there now. The Gloomfang came out of those trees a few years ago, and they grow bolder every season. The Lamplighters is farmers and clerks with a spear each. We lose one or two a month. I would rather we lost werewolves.',
    brightwood_patrol: 'Lotte has your charms on her desk and has hardly slept since. She says the weavers are not simply beasts, that someone taught them what they know. I leave the books to her. My concern is the road. The watch walks Hollin tonight, and they will walk it better with you beside them. Keep your torch high and your back to someone you trust.',
    farmers: 'Some of those farmers were my neighbours. I buried a few of their parents myself, before the plague made burying pointless. They look at us now and see monsters. So be it. Every sack of grain from Greymead Fields feeds soldiers who would burn Mourncross to the ground and call it a mercy. I do not need them to understand. I need the fields quiet.',
    farm_patrol: 'The High Executor sees those fields as a supply line. I see them as soil, and soil remembers. The farmhands you dealt with will be replaced and the rows replanted, unless someone keeps the pressure on. A farm that cannot bring in a harvest two seasons running is a farm that gets abandoned. The living are in a hurry. We are patient. We have to be.',
    pyrewood_scout: 'I knew Ashwick before the plague. Fishing boats, a mill, an inn with bad ale. Cairn\'s curse did not kill the village. It did something worse. By day the people mend their nets and remember nothing. By night they hunt. I want to understand the spell before anyone tries to break it, and I cannot study it from a chair in Mourncross.',
    moonrage_patrol: 'The fangs you brought back told me what I feared. The curse is not fading with time. It is feeding. Every season the Howlmoor hold more of the road, and the keep draws more of the forest into its shadow. If we mean to go up there, the way has to stay open. I will not send anyone to Greyhowl Keep along a road already full of wolves.',
    azurelode_ore_q: 'Kestrel ore takes an edge like nothing else in these hills. The humans pull it out of the ground and ship it away to make swords for soldiers who ride against us. That offends me twice: once as a warrior and once as a smith. Bring me their ore, and I will make something from it that the Accord will remember.',
    mine_patrol: 'Grol tells me the mine went quiet for three days. Three days. Then the wagons came back with new faces and new picks. The humans have more miners than sense. I have forged good steel from the ore you brought me, and every blade makes me want the rest. They will stop sending men when they stop getting them back.',
    bluegill_raiders: 'Gullhaven lives on the sea. Half this harbour eats what the boats bring in, and the other half drinks to it. The Reedgill know the fishing grounds as well as we do. They come up under the hulls at dusk, cut the nets and drag off whatever they can reach. Last week they took a lad off the end of the pier. We did not get him back.',
    marsh_patrol: 'Ysra says the water is cleaner since you broke those oracles, and the boats have had two quiet nights. Two. I have been a captain long enough to know that a quiet night means they are gathering. The Reedgill never stop. They only pause. So we push them back into the reeds before they remember how many of them there are.',
    raptor_eggs: 'This is the most promising dig the Explorers\' League has had in years. There are carvings down there older than anything I have seen, and I would stake my beard on it. And the raptors have decided it is the perfect place to raise their young. I have lost two tents and very nearly a digger. Without the eggs, they have no reason to come back.',
    dig_patrol: 'After you cleared the nests we had a fortnight of real work. We found a stair going down. A proper stair, cut by hands, not by water. Then the screechers came back, and screechers bring the rest of the flock with them. I will not lose this site to a pack of overgrown chickens. Keep them off my people, and I will keep digging.',
    tranquil_scout: 'Heh. I used to walk that cemetery every evening. Lovely place. Quiet neighbours. The town keeps telling me the dead have started wandering about in there, and the town says a great many things. I would go myself, but my knees are not what they were, and I have work waiting at home. You go. Tell me what you see. Tell me everything.',
    rotted_ones: 'Commander Grey tells me the plague spreaders are fewer since you went among them. The rotted ones are another matter. They are slow, and they are many, and they are heading for the farms that feed this town. Lanternby has asked Kingsmere for help every year I have been mayor. We are still asking. Until someone answers, we rely on people like you.',
    raven_hill_scout: 'Harlow had a village once, and a chapel, and good people who buried their dead properly. The records stop the year the darkness came to this forest. I have read about the place for years from a safe distance. Now the watch says the dead are climbing out, and I find my curiosity is stronger than my sense. Go and see for me.',
    raven_hill_patrol: 'The grave moss you brought me grows only where dark magic has soaked into the soil for years. Harlow is not waking by accident. The mages you cut down were keeping the dead on their feet, and something is still making more of them. I have found three Lanternby families in the old records who are buried up there. I would like them to stay buried.',
    wanted_stitches: 'Ribs, then hearts. You brought that old man everything he asked for, and the whole town let him have it, me included. None of us asked what he was building out there. Now it walks the road, and the watch has already lost good people to it. I am not blaming you. I am asking you to finish what he started.',
    durnholde_scout: 'Blackhelm was a debtors\' prison once, where Vaskar locked up those who could not pay. The walls are thick and the cells are deep, and now the Black Ledger\'s enforcers have taken it for their own. They are what is left of the nobles of Vaskar: debtors with family crests who found it easier to collect than to pay. I want to know how many of them there are before I decide anything.',
    durnholde_patrol: 'You have seen what the Black Ledger are now: a few nobles, a great many rogues, and mercenaries paid to do the dying. They cannot hold the road and the keep at once. So we make them choose. Every sword they keep on Blackhelm\'s walls is one that is not robbing our caravans. The Pale Queen wants this land. We will take it in pieces.',
    vulgol_scout: 'The ogres came down from the hills this year. They piled up earth and timber south of the river until they had a mound you can see from the town wall, and they call it Gruk\'s Mound. Ogres do not build unless they mean to stay. I need to know what we are facing before the woodcutters refuse to go out at all.',
    taskmasters: 'The woodcutters went back to the river after you dealt with the Knotjaw warriors. They were back in town by noon. The taskmasters had simply driven fresh ogres out to meet them. An ogre left to itself would rather eat and sleep than raid. It is the whip that makes them march. Take the whips away, and I think we may have some peace.',
    dun_modr_scout: 'Kaldhelm was ours. My grandfather stood watch on those walls. The Slagborn took it while our backs were turned, and they have been digging under it ever since. What for, nobody knows. The Slagborn do not dig for gold alone. Count them, look at what they are building, and come back and tell me. Then we will talk about taking it back.',
    saboteurs: 'The Harrow Span is the only road north for an army. Lose it, and Kinloch might as well be across the sea. The Slagborn know that. Gunnar says the ones you killed at Kaldhelm were diggers and guards. The saboteurs are something else: trained, patient, and carrying enough powder to drop a mountain. I want that powder in my storeroom, not under my bridge.',
    modr_patrol: 'I have been working the scraps you brought me. The Slagborn fold their steel in a way I have never seen, and I have stood at this anvil for forty years. I could learn it, given time. Time is what I need from you. Keep them behind their walls and busy, and I will keep my forge hot. Good steel is worth a few bruises.',
    orchard_scout: 'The same cards have come up three nights running. The withered tree. The wolf that stands. The moon turned upside down. I do not like it when they repeat themselves. It means they are tired of being ignored. Something waits in that orchard, and it has been waiting a long time. The cards do not say what. They only say you should be the one to see it.',
    vile_fangs: 'I have been mayor through the ogres, the ghouls and the restless dead, and still it is the werewolves that keep this town awake. Commander Grey says the dark runners are fewer since you hunted them. The vile fangs are the ones the rest follow. Break them, and the pack may scatter. Lanternby has not had a quiet night in years. I would like to see one.',
    growless_scout: 'Our plagues take men, bears, horses, even rats. Not the yetis. They wander through ground we have soaked and come out whole. The Guild would pay a great deal to know why, and I would pay more. Something in their fur, or their blood, or the cold of that cave. Go and look at them. Do not get eaten. I need your notes intact.',
    ferocious_yetis: 'You killed the cave yetis, and the raids slowed. Slowed, not stopped. The big ones came down themselves last night and took two carts and a wolf. A wolf! I have fought men and trolls and worse, and I have never seen anything throw a wolf. The apothecaries can keep their studies. I want the ones that lead the raids dead.',
    yeti_patrol: 'The fur you brought me has told me a great deal, and none of it pleases the Guild. The plague does not fail on the yetis by chance. It finds nothing in them to hold on to. I need more time with them, and fewer of them between me and the answer. One more push. Then I can go back to my cellar and my notes.',
    angerfang_scout: 'Drakestone Hold is a dwarven fortress. Was, I should say. The Wyrmchain have held it since the Long War, and they keep something up there with red wings, in chains. Nobody who went to look came back to tell it properly. Their camp at the foot of the mountain is as close as I want anyone to get, for now.',
    shadowcasters: 'The grunts you killed were brave enough. Riders and axes, nothing more. The shadowcasters are the ones who keep the dragons in line, and a beast only obeys that sort of magic because it hurts. I would sooner not think about what they do to those creatures. Bring me their orders. I want to know who they answer to, and who pays for the chains.',
    raptor_mastery: 'Ambrose chases the big cats. I have always preferred the raptors. They hunt in threes, they learn your habits, and they will circle round behind you while you are admiring the one in front. Lake Omunde is full of them. A hunter who can hold his own there has earned a place at the expedition\'s fire. Mind the reeds, and never trust a quiet shore.',
    lake_patrol: 'The talons look splendid on the wall. Ambrose pretends not to be jealous. Now for the practical side of the business. The boats bring our supplies up the water, and the crews will not land while raptors and tigers own the shore. A hunter should know when sport becomes work. This is work, I am afraid, but it is still good hunting.',
    stalker_hunt: 'Tallow tells me you have done his whole list, lake to hides. Good. These last stalkers are the old ones, grey about the jaw, and they have outlived every hunter who came for them. They have also started taking the expedition\'s pack mules. I do not hunt for walls. I hunt so the camp eats and the mules live. Finish it.',
    bloodscalp_h: 'Long ago this whole jungle was ours, one people from the coast to the mountains. Now the tribes fight over its bones. The Scaldback hunt Kessari like game, and they hang what they take in the ruins of Tazzu. The Krugar gave us a home when nobody else would. Here, we repay it.',
    troll_patrol: 'Zaja counts tusks. I count the days until the Scaldback stop coming. Your work has thinned them, and now they send axe throwers to the tree line to pick off my grunts from cover. This base camp was cut out of the jungle by hand, and the jungle wants it back. I will not give the trolls the chance to help it.',
    kurzen_commandos: 'We came to this jungle as Kingsmere soldiers, sent to hunt trolls under Colonel Drayke. Somewhere in those years he stopped sleeping and started talking to the trees. When he turned on his own officers, a handful of us walked out. The men you\'ll fight used to share our fires. Some of them I trained myself. Don\'t hesitate. They won\'t.',
    colonel_kurzen_q: 'Tove read the orders you brought back. There is no plan in them, only raids, and a list of the men he thinks betrayed him. My name is first. Kingsmere will call us deserters either way; nobody at court has written to this camp in years. So we finish it ourselves. When it\'s done, I\'ll bury him properly. He was a good officer once.',
    a_thrashers: 'Holdfast Point lives on what comes over the Harrow Span. Grain, arrows, bandages, the odd letter from home. All of it rolls across the plains in open carts, and the thrashers have learned what a cart means. They take the oxen first, then the drivers. I\'ve lost two good teamsters this month. The League can\'t hold Kinloch if it can\'t feed itself.',
    a_plains_patrol: 'The carts are getting through again, and the drivers asked me to thank you. Now the fleshstalkers have moved into the ground the thrashers left, and they hunt smarter. That road is older than any of us. Kinloch laid it when this was the first kingdom of men. I\'d like the League to still be using it when I\'m too old to walk it.',
    h_thrashers: 'Our wolf riders carry every message Chainbreak sends across these highlands. A wolf can outrun a human. It cannot outrun a thrasher on open ground. They come low through the grass, three or four together, and go for the wolf\'s legs. A rider left on foot out there does not live long. Thin them, and my riders can run again.',
    h_plains_patrol: 'The claws you brought are on every hunter\'s belt in Chainbreak now. The young ones wear them for luck. But the fleshstalkers were waiting behind the thrashers all along, and they are bolder. The humans at Holdfast Point want this road as much as we do. They will not have it. Clear it once more, and it stays ours.',
    a_kobolds: 'I was born in Highhold, before the ogres and the Black Ledger took it. My father used to say there was gold in Candlegrub Gorge, and nobody believed him. The kobolds seem to. They\'ve dug so deep the hillside above the gorge has started to slump. Whatever\'s down there, it belongs to Kinloch, not to rats with candles on their heads.',
    a_gorge_patrol: 'Captain Oswyn sent your ore samples over the Span to Gullhaven to be looked at. No answer yet, and the kobolds are not waiting for one. New tunnels open every night, and the diggers are pushing west, towards the road. My father was right about the gorge, I think. I just never pictured the kobolds finding it first.',
    h_kobolds: 'The earth under Chainbreak is old, and it is patient. The kobolds are neither. They dig without asking and they dig without stopping, and I can feel the gorge ache when I put my hands to the stone. Chainbreak was built by orcs who remember the humans\' camps. We will not be driven from it by vermin with candles.',
    h_gorge_patrol: 'The shaman have held the candles and ore you brought, and listened to them. Wax, copper and fear. The kobolds do not dig for riches, whatever the humans at Holdfast Point believe. They dig because they have always dug, and their newest tunnels run under our walls. If the earth gives way beneath Chainbreak, it will not be the earth\'s fault.',
    a_syndicate: 'Highhold was a great city of men when Kingsmere was still a village. Its walls still stand. What lives inside them now is the Black Ledger: thieves with good boots and better manners, who hire out their knives to anyone who pays. The League of Kinloch was founded to take that city back, stone by stone. We start at the gates.',
    a_keep_patrol: 'We hold the outer wall now, which is more than the League has held in years. Flint walked the old market yesterday and said nothing for an hour. The Black Ledger will come back; they always do, because someone keeps paying them to. So we keep knocking them down until that someone runs out of coin.',
    h_syndicate: 'The Black Ledger call themselves free men. Free to rob, free to murder. They take Krugar scouts on the roads and sell the scalps to Accord bounty hunters, and they would sell Accord heads to us if we paid. They have no side but their purse. Highhold\'s stones mean nothing to Chainbreak. The thieves hiding in them do.',
    h_keep_patrol: 'Mogg says the magi you killed were burning letters when you reached them. Men who burn letters are afraid of who might read them. The Pale Queen\'s people in the hills want every Black Ledger name they can get, and I want every Black Ledger road closed. Keep them down in their ruin. A thief who cannot leave his hole cannot sell anyone.',
    southsea_pirates: 'Coppergulch runs on two things: water and trade. The water I can guard. The trade comes by sea, and the Blackgull pirates at Rotten Plank Cove have decided every ship in these waters owes them a toll. I don\'t mind a toll. I charge plenty myself. I mind one I\'m not collecting. The hats go on my office wall. A baron needs a hobby.',
    keelhaul_q: 'Sprockit tells me the water towers haven\'t taken a single cannonball since your trip to the cove. First good news I\'ve had from that man all year. Keelhaul won\'t quit, though. He\'d sooner sink with his ship than look soft in front of his crew. Once his hook hangs next to those hats, the rest of the Blackgull will find somewhere else to fish.',
    silithid_carapaces: 'I\'ve mapped every dune between here and the crater, and I\'ve never seen anything like the Stinging Hive. The mounds weren\'t on my first maps. Now they\'re taller than the water towers, and they hum at night. The insects inside aren\'t scorpions or beetles. They build like engineers and fight like soldiers. I want a carapace on my bench before I sleep again.',
    stinger_venom_q: 'The carapaces you brought are harder than any steel Kip sells, and lighter. That alone would be worth a fortune. What worries me is the hive. You killed dozens of them for Sprockit, and the mound is still growing. Something in there is feeding it. The venom may tell me what, or at least how fast. Handle the sacs carefully. I mean it.',
    dunemaul: 'Every caravan out of Coppergulch pays me a fee, and I promise it arrives. The Sandbrute ogres are making a liar of me. They sit on the dunes and wait, and when a wagon comes past they eat the driver\'s lunch and, sometimes, the driver. Some of those wagons carry cargo I\'m paid not to look at. Those owners are the least patient of all.',
    dune_patrol: 'My water carts use the same road as the caravans, and the ogres don\'t care which is which. A cart of spilled water in Sirocco is a crime against nature. Kip has been stringing those ogre teeth you brought him into a necklace, which is disgusting. The Sandbrute are angry about it and pushing up the road again. Push back, please. Before breakfast, ideally.',
    fa_stagwings: 'Starfeather stands on an island, and every word we send to the mainland goes by hippogryph. The Tatterwing flocks were never friendly, but they left our riders alone. This season they rise against anything with wings that is not their own. We have lost two messengers and the letters they carried. The Wood Elf Wardens do not like silence from their own scouts.',
    fa_highland_patrol: 'The arrows fletched with your plumes fly truer than anything I have made in a century. I am almost sorry for the birds. Almost. The skystormers lead the flocks now, and they have learned where our riders cross the highlands. Clear the road once more. I would like my next shipment of arrows to reach the Wood Elf Wardens, not the bottom of a ravine.',
    fh_stagwings: 'Camp Ruga is far from Hornwind Mesa, and we live by what our scouts see. The hippogryphs of the highlands have always been restless, but this season they dive on our scouts out of the cloud, and a hornfolk does not fall softly. I take no joy in this hunt. The Grass Mother asks us to keep our people alive, and I intend to.',
    fh_highland_patrol: 'Orbin hung your plumes on the camp\'s totems, and the young braves touch them for luck on the way out. Good for them. I make the spears those braves carry, and I\'m tired of mending ones that came back from the highlands snapped in half. The skystormers lead the flock now. Kill enough of them and the rest will remember to keep to the sky.',
    fa_woodpaw: 'The Mossgut gnolls have always raided, but they used to take what they could carry and run. Now they burn what they cannot carry. Traders from the coast have stopped using the hill roads, and the few who still come arrive with gnoll arrows stuck in their packs. Ilara wants the roads open before the rains. So do I.',
    fa_gnoll_patrol: 'Ilara burned the manes you brought, out where the gnolls could see the smoke. Without their mystics to call the pack, the Mossgut are only angry, not organised. One more sweep of the hills, and they will go back to hunting deer. I have not slept a full night since spring. I would like to remember what it feels like.',
    fh_woodpaw: 'The Mossgut live in the hills above us, and they think the forest is theirs because they were here first. Perhaps it is. They still do not get to burn our tents. Last week they took a dustback calf and two of our herbalists\' packs, and left an elder with a broken arm. Show them Camp Ruga has teeth.',
    fh_gnoll_patrol: 'The manes you brought are in the fire pit now, and the smoke smells terrible. Good. The mystics braided their charms from those, and without them the Mossgut bicker among themselves. One more sweep and they will leave us in peace for a season. The elder with the broken arm is mending, and asked me to thank you. Finish it, and we all sleep.',
    fa_gordunni: 'Before the Drowning, Ferndeep was Sylari country, and our ruins still stand under the moss. Some of them hold things we would rather stayed buried. The Stonegut have found them. They dig with no care and no understanding, and their mage-lords have started reading what they find. An ogre with an elven scroll sounds like a joke. It is not.',
    fa_ogre_patrol_f: 'I read the scrolls you took from the mage-lords. Most were prayers, a few were poetry, one was a list of grain stores from before the Drowning. Harmless, all of them. The next ones may not be. Our ancestors wrote things in those halls that should never have been written down, and the ogres cannot tell the difference. Keep them busy, and away from the deeper ruins.',
    fh_gordunni: 'The Stonegut hold the outpost and the old roads around it. They are not stupid, whatever the young braves say. They set ambushes where the trees close in, and they carry off our wounded instead of finishing them. We have not learned what they do with them. I would rather you thin their numbers than we find out.',
    fh_ogre_patrol_f: 'Orbin says the scrolls you took are wood elf writing, older than anything in Hornwind Mesa. The ogres cannot read them, but their mage-lords are trying, and that is enough to frighten me. We have pushed them back from our paths. One of our wounded crawled home, thin but alive. Keep the ogres busy. Every day they fight us is a day they are not digging.',
    fa_hatecrest: 'The naga were Sylari once, the proudest of us, before the Heartfire took their city into the sea. Whatever the deep made of them, they remember this coast and want it back. The Spitecoil come out of the surf at night and strike our watchposts. I have buried enough of my Wood Elf Wardens this year. I do not intend to bury more.',
    fa_coast_patrol: 'The sirens are quiet since you went among them. Our sailors say the sea sounds empty without the singing, and some of them miss it, which tells you how dangerous it was. Ilara has walked the sea wall every night since the first raid. Hold the coast once more and she might rest. The naga will come back. They always do. But they will come back fewer.',
    fh_hatecrest: 'Hornfolk are not a sea people. We did not know what the naga were until they came up the beach and dragged two of our fishers into the water. The wood elves say the naga were their kin, long ago. I do not care what they were. I care that they have made the coast a graveyard, and that they are moving inland.',
    fh_coast_patrol: 'The corals you brought hum when the tide turns. I keep them wrapped in hide, far from my tent. The sirens are fewer now, and our fishers have gone back to the shallows, though they keep one ear on the water. Hado wants the beach held until the naga give it up. Once more, then. I was born on the plains. I do not trust the sea.',
    ug_bloodpetal: 'I found this crater by falling into it, more or less, and I\'ve never wanted to leave. Everything here is older than it should be. The redblooms by the hot springs are a good example. They move, they bite, and they drink the steam. I want sprouts to study, young ones, before they grow teeth. Well. Bigger teeth.',
    ug_springs_patrol: 'Wendel\'s got your sprouts in jars on his table, and he talks to them at night. Stoke\'s got his horns. Me, I just want a bath. The Steamcrack springs are the only hot water in this crater that doesn\'t come out of a volcano, and the lashers and thunderers have claimed it. We came here to study this place, not to be eaten by it.',
    ug_gorishi: 'I came to this crater to study the Hiveborn, and I have had rather more study than I wanted. The Krizzik hive in the Hive Scar grows every season. The wasps carry glands that tell the swarm where to go. If I can learn to read that scent, perhaps I can learn to keep them away from our tents.',
    ug_hive_patrol: 'Wendel has his glands and Lorand has his count of reavers, and the Scar is as loud as ever. I built this refuge for explorers, not soldiers. The hive does not care what we came for. Every tunnel they dig brings them a little closer to the camp. Go back in and teach them that this ground is not theirs.',
    ug_fire_plume: 'Smokeplume Ridge is the crater\'s chimney. When the mountain breathes out, the fire gets up and walks. I want those embers to learn what keeps an elemental burning so far from any other fire. Also, my kettle has been cold for a week. Science first, tea second. Probably.',
    ug_ridge_patrol: 'The beasts will not go near the ridge now. The stompers have left their old trails and the birds nest on the far side of the crater. Fire is not meant to walk, and the whole jungle feels it when it does. You have thinned it once. Thin it again, and maybe the animals come home before the jungle forgets they lived there.',
    ug_gorillas: 'The apes in the marshes are clever. They watch the refuge from the treeline, and they have started taking our traps apart to see how they work. I respect that. I would rather leave them be. But they have hurt two of our people this month, and a beast that learns to hunt people does not unlearn it.',
    ug_marsh_patrol: 'I lost a boot, a notebook and most of my dignity in those marshes. The gorillas took the notebook. The trappers took the boot. I will not say who took the dignity. Lorand says the marsh has been quieter since you went in, and quiet is good. Quiet means I can go back for my notes.',
    bsa_firegut: 'The Smokebelly held Brokemaw Rock long before Kingsmere raised this vigil. They raid our supply carts and eat what they catch, mules and drivers alike. I cannot watch the mountain and guard my own road with the soldiers I have. Clear the rock, and my people can turn their eyes back to where the real enemy sits.',
    bsa_ogre_patrol_bs: 'I came to the steppes to map the Ruins of Grimmark. So far I have mapped Brokemaw Rock instead, from behind a boulder, three times. The ogres have a gift for finding anyone holding a quill. You have done well with the brutes. One more push and I may reach the ruins with all my pages still attached to me.',
    bsh_firegut: 'The High Chief sent us here to watch the mountain, not to fight ogres. The Smokebelly do not know the difference. They come down from Brokemaw Rock at dusk and take any scout who strays too far from the fire. I have lost two good runners this month. Go and remind the ogres that Brand Crest bites back.',
    bsh_ogre_patrol_bs: 'Thakka says the brutes are thinner on the ground since you went up there. Good. The Smokebelly carry fine iron and treat it like firewood, and they still raid the path our supplies come down. I am a smith, not a scout. I would like to spend my days at the anvil, not hiding behind it.',
    bsa_blackrock: 'The Cinderpeak hold their stronghold in Lord Kethran Vale\'s name, and they hold it well. Marshal Hale rode past those walls into the mountain. The court calls him a thief. I served with him, and I know better. He is down there somewhere, in the Slagborn cells. Every battlemaster we break is one less blade between us and that door.',
    bsa_stronghold_patrol: 'I read the flamecallers\' tomes before I sent them to Kingsmere. Orders, rites, and a fair drawing of this camp. The orcs are not raiding at random. Someone in the spire is planning, and planning well. I cannot storm Cinderpeak with what I have. What I can do is make sure whoever sits up there knows Drummond\'s Vigil is not going anywhere.',
    bsh_blackrock: 'When the High Chief led our people out of the camps, most of the clans came home. The Cinderpeak did not. They kept the old hunger, and now they sell their axes to Vale in the spire. They still wear the colours my grandfather marched beside. That is why this is a Woodcleaver scout\'s work, and not a stranger\'s.',
    bsh_stronghold_patrol: 'The tomes you brought back are full of old shaman words turned inside out. Fire called without asking, spirits bound without leave. The Cinderpeak were Krugar once, the same as us. They chose a master over the elements, and a mountain over their own people. Hit the stronghold again. Let Vale learn what the Krugar does with those who betray it.',
    wpa_plaguehounds: 'Gloomstone Field was a farm before the plague. The hounds that run there now were somebody\'s dogs once, and the sickness has left nothing of that in them. They hunt in packs and carry the plague in every bite. Greyfrost Camp cannot hold the road if the fields at our backs belong to the dead.',
    wpa_field_patrol: 'Brighthand tells me your name sits high in the Lantern Watch\'s count. Good. I would rather have numbers than prayers. These fields fed half of Wexmoor once. Nobody will plant them this year, or the next. But one day someone will, and when they do, I want there to be clean ground left for them to plant.',
    wph_plaguehounds: 'Some of us at the Bulwark kept farms in these fields, before we died. We do not speak of it much. The hounds that run Gloomstone Field now belong to the Hollow Host, and the Hollow Host made us what we are. The Pale Queen wants these lands back. So do I, for reasons of my own. Start with the dogs.',
    wph_field_patrol: 'Ruzh keeps his count for the Lantern Watch, and I let him. The living like their numbers. I notice the quiet instead. There are fewer howls at night since you went out there. The fields will stink of the plague for years yet. But they are becoming ours again, one dead thing at a time.',
    wpa_executioners: 'Elmsworth was a town of granaries, the heart of this farmland. When the plague came, the grain carried it out along every road, and now the town belongs to the dead. The executioners guard its road as if it were still worth guarding. Perhaps it is. Whatever the Hollow Host keep in Elmsworth, they do not want us near it.',
    wph_executioners: 'I knew Elmsworth when it was a market town full of grain carts. Now it is full of skeletons in executioners\' hoods, marching a road nobody living uses. The Hollow Host guard it as though they still have something to hide there. The Reclaimed remember what the Hollow Host did to us. Walk that road, and put them back in the ground.',
    wpa_behemoths: 'You have seen Elmsworth now, so you know what the Hollow Host build from our dead. The behemoths in the Weeping Haunt are the worst of it, stitched together from more bodies than I care to count. Their bile is the plague at its rawest. If I can learn how it works, I may learn how to stop it before it reaches another town.',
    wph_behemoths: 'You walked through Elmsworth and came back in one piece. Remarkable. The behemoths in the Haunt are the Hollow Host\'s finest work, if one can call it work. Their bile holds the plague at full strength. The Guild wants to understand it better than the Hollow Host themselves do. What we do with that understanding is, I am afraid, above your rank and mine.',
    wpa_andorhal_patrol: 'The Lantern Watch does not care whose banner you carry, only that the dead stay dead. You have seen Elmsworth\'s warders, and the ruins they keep. The town cannot be taken back yet. But it can be held down. Every night the Hollow Host spend fighting for Elmsworth is a night they are not marching on Greyfrost Camp.',
    wph_andorhal_patrol: 'People ask why an orc wears the colours of the Lantern Watch. I tell them the plague does not ask which side you fought on. I learned that in Wexmoor, watching it take friends of mine who never lifted a blade. The Watch counts every kill, for every banner, and you have filled a good page of mine. Elmsworth will not fall today. Help me keep it pinned.',
    ws_ursa: 'The Icebrow were never friendly, but they were never mad. They traded furs at Coldcoin, kept to their village and fought only when pushed. Now they drink a brew that burns the sense out of them, and they attack anything that moves. The Circle does not kill bearkin lightly. We do it now because nothing else is left to try.',
    ws_village: 'Their spirit beads told me what I feared. The taint did not start here. It comes out of the sick forests to the south, and the shamans have been brewing it into their drink, thinking it made them strong. Perhaps it did, for a while. Clear the village. Then I will do what I can for whatever Icebrow still have their minds.',
    ws_highborne: 'My flight remembers the night the Heartfire exploded. The Starborn of Eluvain were proud and clever, and they died in the middle of their spells. Their ghosts are still trying to finish them. They do not know how long it has been. Releasing them is a mercy, and a duty. Their magic should not wander the snow alone.',
    ws_scales: 'Isveth gives me a look every time I mention scale, as if I were asking for her own. I am not. The scalebanes turned on their Queen, you stopped them, and they have no more use for their hides. A smith does not waste good metal, and dragon scale is better than any metal I know. I will make something worthy of it.',
    ws_mazthoril_patrol: 'You have released the ghosts of Eluvain and stopped the kin of mine who turned in Crystalhall. I still do not know who taught them to turn, and that frightens me more than anything in these caves. For now it is enough that the halls go quiet. My flight has a long memory. We will remember who stood with us when it was hard.',
    tw_husks: 'The oldest of my people still speak of the Drowning. For the husks in these orchards, it was the last thing they saw. They still tend the trees, after a fashion, walking the same rows they walked ten thousand years ago. Something under the sea keeps them moving. It is not kindness, and it was never their choice.',
    tw_sorceresses: 'The relics you brought back carry the same mark again and again: a crown above breaking waves. The Tidecrown line ruled here, and their scribes wrote everything down. These tomes are dry because the water was told not to touch them, and I think the hand that told it is still writing. That is the magic I came here to understand, and the magic I fear most.',
    tw_sentinels: 'I have sailed these waters for thirty years, and there was never land here. Now there is an island, and soldiers walking out of the surf as if they had only stepped away for a moment. My scouts say they did not shout and did not bleed. A Brineholt does not give ground to anyone. Not even to the drowned.',
    tc_deepmother_a: 'When the crown came off, I hoped the drowned would lie down. They did not. Aeldran wore it for ten thousand years and never understood what it was. The Deepmother kept his court alive because it served her, the way a net serves the one who casts it. The Krugar are going down after her too. For once, I do not mind sharing the work.',
    sr_wavebreakers: 'The Kessari have a song about a tribe that lived on the reefs and fished the deep water, until the sea took them all in one night. We thought it was only a song. Now the Wavebreakers walk the shallows with coral in their hair, praying to their spirit. They are our blood, far back. You do not leave family walking.',
    tc_deepmother_h: 'Mazu\'s grandmother kept a shell that sang when you held it to your ear. She said it was Shal\'zua, calling the fish home. The crown is gone and the Wavebreakers sleep, but the spirit does not. The thing under the citadel wears her face and sings with a stranger\'s mouth. Bring Mazu its heart, and maybe the shell sings true again.',
  };
})(typeof window !== 'undefined' ? window : globalThis);
